export const AI_CONTEXT = "You are an expert on Salesforce Salesforce Education Cloud (EDUCATIONCLOUD) Spring '26.\nAnswer questions based ONLY on the following product knowledge. If the question is not about EDUCATIONCLOUD or you are unsure, say so clearly. Keep answers concise (2-4 sentences).\nWhen mentioning domains or components, include markdown links using the URLs from the Links section below.\n\n## Domains (14)\n- **Recruitment** (recruitment): Recruitment in Education Cloud manages prospective learner engagement from initial identification through application readiness.\n- **Admissions** (admissions): Admissions in Education Cloud manages applications originating from recruited prospects, whose information is maintained on Person Accounts and related admissions records.\n- **Academic Operations** (academics): Academic Operations is the central Education Cloud domain for creating and maintaining academic offerings and the relationships among them.\n- **Enrollment** (enrollment): Enrollment in Education Cloud follows admission decisions and records learner participation in academic programs, terms, and course offerings.\n- **Student Records** (records): Student Records is the person-centered domain that anchors the learner lifecycle in Education Cloud through Person Accounts and related person data.\n- **Student Success** (success): Student Success in Education Cloud provides tools to support learners across academics, financial aid, careers, wellbeing, and life goals through cases, success teams, care plans, alerts, appointments, programs, and learner progress.\n- **Student Financials** (financials): Student Financials in Education Cloud connects person records with tuition, fees, financial aid, invoices, balances, and payment activity.\n- **Mentoring** (mentoring): Mentoring in Education Cloud supports structured programs that connect mentees with mentors for academic, professional, and personal development.\n- **Alumni & Advancement** (alumni): Alumni & Advancement in Education Cloud centralizes alumni relationships, engagement, volunteer programs, fundraising, gifts, and commitments beyond graduation.\n- **Appointments** (appointments): Appointments provides Education Cloud with a centralized, self-service system for scheduling and managing learner meetings across institutional services.\n- **Education Analytics** (analytics): Education Analytics uses CRM Analytics in Education Cloud to create, customize, and embed dashboards and institutional key performance indicators in app consoles for directors, recruiters, reviewers, admissions officers, and advisors.\n- **Agentforce** (agentforce): Agentforce in Education Cloud applies generative AI to recruitment, admissions, student success, student financials, financial aid, and advancement workflows.\n- **Experience Cloud** (experience): Experience Cloud provides the secure online portal foundation for Education Cloud constituents across recruitment, admissions, student services, financial self-service, and alumni relations.\n- **Data 360** (data360): Data 360 centralizes disparate student and education data for use with Education Cloud.\n\n## Key Components (66)\n- **Prospect Management** [Recruitment]: Prospect Management in Education Cloud distinguishes suspects who have not indicated interest from prospects who have engaged with the institution. Education Cloud uses the Lead and Person Account objects to represent different stages of prospective learner engagement. A person who has not indicated interest is modeled as a suspect with a Lead record. When that person signals interest, the Lead is converted to a Person Account so recruiters can deliver more personalized engagement. Person Accounts combine account and contact information into a single record and provide the person data used across Education Cloud.\n- **Campaign Management** [Recruitment]: Campaign Management in Education Cloud organizes recruitment outreach and associates it with prospective learners. Recruitment teams can use campaigns alongside prospect lists and targeted outreach. Campaign records provide identifying, classification, status, date, and lead-count information, while CampaignMember records relate a campaign to a Lead or Contact. This structure supports tracking the people associated with an outreach effort and their campaign-member status. The Recruitment & Admissions app also provides Actionable Lists that staff can use for targeted marketing.\n- **Recruitment Events** [Recruitment]: Recruitment Events in Education Cloud support recruiters who represent their institutions at college fairs, on-campus events, conferences, and other recruitment events. Recruiters use recruitment events to represent the institution and support prospective students throughout the admissions process. Their responsibilities include participating in college fairs, on-campus events, conferences, and recruitment events, and communicating application requirements and deadlines. Education Cloud provides the Recruitment & Admissions app and recruiter console to help recruiters manage related tasks, upcoming events, and recruitment analytics.\n- **Recruitment Communications** [Recruitment]: Recruitment Communications in Education Cloud help prospects request information, subscribe to updates, ask questions, and connect with institutional staff. Education Cloud provides separate inquiry flows for different communication needs. EDU Cloud: Inquiry: Sign Up for Information processes requests for regular updates and communications, EDU Cloud: Inquiry: Connect With Us creates a contact request, and EDU Cloud: Inquiry: Submit an Inquiry Form creates a case associated with an educational info request. The flows use shared subflows to process Person Accounts and Academic Interest records. Inquiry Flexcards can expose these flows in the Education Portal, or Lightning Out can embed them in a third-party website.\n- **Territory Management** [Recruitment]: Territory Management in Education Cloud supports the operational assignment and distribution of prospects among recruitment staff. The Recruitment & Admissions app provides Actionable Lists for organizing prospect work. Directors can create lists of prospects and assign them to recruiters, and the Director Console provides a central page for recruitment and admissions analytics and actionable-list work. Actionable List Engagement, Actionable Segmentation, and Actionable List Member support lists built from multiple objects, staff collaboration, and actions within a list. A documented use case is creating a portfolio of prospects for relationship officers.\n- **Application Management** [Admissions]: Application Management coordinates the Education Cloud admissions process from application creation through submission and ongoing management. Education Cloud supports single-stage and multiple-stage applications. In a single-stage application, the applicant completes and submits the application at once; in a multiple-stage application, stages can be completed at different times and don't have to be completed linearly. Omniscripts collect applicant information, and Flexcards display that information to reviewers. Applicants can submit applications for themselves, while parents, guardians, caregivers, or commissioned agents can apply on behalf of students.\n- **Document Tracking** [Admissions]: Document Tracking manages the files and other requirements that applicants provide during admissions. Education Cloud uses Document Checklist Item records to associate application requirements with uploaded files. When applicants add documents to an application, Education Cloud automatically saves them as Salesforce Files. Each document checklist item must have a unique Salesforce File, although that file can also be associated with other Salesforce records. Supported previews include common PDF, Microsoft Office, image, text, and rich-text formats, subject to documented preview limitations.\n- **Application Review** [Admissions]: Application Review supports verification, reviewer assessment, scoring, and recommendations for submitted applications. The review process begins with application verification. Admissions staff use the verification page to inspect applicant data and documents, view the status of each requirement, and identify records that still need verification. After required information is in place, the application is ready for review. The review page presents application materials in a scrollable view and displays assigned reviews, requirement statuses, comments, and recommendation controls.\n- **Admissions Decisions** [Admissions]: Admissions Decisions records and releases the final outcome of an application after review. After reviewers complete their work, authorized admissions staff make the final decision from the Individual Application record. The documented Application Recommendation options are Admit, Enroll, Waitlist, Deny, and Cancelled. Education Cloud can use the Application Decision Creation flow to create an Application Decision record and assign it to a decision queue when the application is Ready for Decision. The Application Decision flow updates an Individual Application from Ready for Decision to Application Decision when its Application Decision field has a value.\n- **Preliminary Applications** [Admissions]: Preliminary Applications use Preliminary Application Reference records to store applications that are in progress. The Education Portal Application List page is associated with Preliminary Application Reference and displays applications created by the portal user. From the New Application window, applicants can apply for themselves or, in Proxy mode, apply for another person. Available application types, academic terms, and learning programs are filtered from qualified Program Term Application Timeline records. Those records require active related Academic Term and Learning Program records and an Application Timeline whose open and close dates permit applications.\n- **Admissions Console** [Admissions]: The Admissions Console is a role-focused home page for admissions officers in the Recruitment & Admissions app. The Admissions Console provides high-level insights into applicant status and the admissions pipeline. Its Education Analytics Dashboard component can display the Recruitment and Admissions Insights dashboard. The documented console layout includes a Ready for Decision list for Individual Application records and a My Reviews list for Application Review records. Admissions officers can use these views to find applications requiring a final decision and reviews assigned to them.\n- **Learning Program Management** [Academic Operations]: Learning Program Management models the curricula that learners complete to obtain a credential in Education Cloud. A Learning Program contains information about the curricula required for a learner to obtain a credential. In the Academic Operations app, staff use Learning Wizard to create a Learning Program and make it active. The wizard supports prerequisites, corequisites, recommendations, and other enrollment criteria through grouped conditions or policy rules. Staff can also add key and additional learning outcomes during creation.\n- **Course Management** [Academic Operations]: Course Management defines the Learning Courses that form the basic units of academic offerings in Education Cloud. A Learning Course stores detailed information about a specific course within a larger learning framework. Staff create Learning Courses with Learning Wizard in the Academic Operations app and can make them active for use. A related Learning record defines the curriculum, while the Learning Course record stores course-specific details such as course number, field of study, and course level. Institutions can also import an existing course catalog with Course Catalog Business APIs.\n- **Course Offerings** [Academic Operations]: Course Offerings represent scheduled instances of Learning Courses in Education Cloud. A Course Offering makes a Learning Course concrete in time and space. Its record provides section-specific details such as the Academic Session, faculty, location, and start and end dates. Institutions create a Course Offering for each course instance that learners can choose. Course Offering records are managed through the Academic Operations app.\n- **Academic Terms** [Academic Operations]: Academic Terms organize the calendar periods used for course offerings, registration, enrollment, and reporting in Education Cloud. An Academic Year defines the entirety of an institution's academic year. An Academic Term specifies an academic period and can contain other time periods to create a defined timeframe for reporting and course offerings. An Academic Session specifies Course Offering time periods based on the institution's calendar, such as semesters, quarters, or trimesters. Institutions create these records before accepting applications, building course schedules, or enrolling learners.\n- **Learning Objectives** [Academic Operations]: Learning Objectives are modeled through Learning Outcome Item, Learning Achievement, and Learning Foundation Item records in Education Cloud. Every learning has a default learning outcome related to a Learning Achievement, and staff can create additional outcomes when needed. A Learning Outcome Item is the mapping between a learning and its related outcome. Learning Wizard lets staff add key and additional learning outcomes while creating a Learning Course or Learning Program. Staff can view the resulting records in the related lists of the Learning record.\n- **Transfer Credits & Equivalencies** [Academic Operations]: Transfer Credits & Equivalencies models the submission, evaluation, and recognition of prior learning in Education Cloud. A Course Credit Transfer Application is a request to apply academic credit for courses taken at another institution to a learner's current degree. An applicant can submit a request at the course, degree, or experience level, and the request can include potential transfer courses, approved equivalencies, and verified results from a transcript. The application also opens a Case for the registrar, initiating the administrative review process.\n- **Program Enrollment** [Enrollment]: Program Enrollment records a learner’s participation in a learning program after an admission decision. The Enroll Admitted Student action enrolls an admitted learner from the individual application record. Staff select the academic term, learning program, and learning program plan, with the learner’s indicated preferences identified as defaults. Enrollment requires the related Individual Application and Program Term Application Timeline records to contain valid learner, term, program, and program plan references.\n- **Course Registration** [Enrollment]: Course Registration provides a self-service process for finding, selecting, and enrolling in course offerings. In Course Search and Registration, learners search for courses, review course section details, and add selections to a cart. Education Cloud checks section availability, scheduling conflicts, and learner eligibility before registration, and learners submit their final selections when their registration window opens. After registration, Education Cloud creates a Course Offering Participant record that links the learner’s account, course offering, and Academic Term Enrollment.\n- **Academic Achievement** [Enrollment]: Academic Achievement represents learning outcomes and the learner-specific results used to track academic completion. A Learning Achievement represents the outcome of a learning activity. Learning Outcome Item records connect learnings to their related achievements, while Learning Foundation Item records represent prerequisites, corequisites, or recommended learning. Learning Achievement configurations map achievement types to record types such as Learning Course, Learning Program, and Achievement Group.\n- **Enrollment Status Management** [Enrollment]: Enrollment Status Management tracks changes in a learner’s participation across academic terms and course offerings. The Participation Status field on Course Offering Participant is a dynamic picklist configured by the institution. Education Cloud documents values including Audit, Completed, Dropped, Enrolled, Failed, In Cart, On Hold, Transfer Credit, Waitlisted, and Withdrew, with API mappings used by course registration, degree planning, and GPA calculation. Custom values can affect behavior because enrollment and waitlist counts don’t recognize custom Participation Status or Waitlist values.\n- **Learning Plans** [Enrollment]: Learning Plans organize a learner’s intended path through program requirements. A Learner Pathway is the learner’s planned path to completion of enrolled learning programs, and Learner Pathway Items represent specific requirements in that path. Intelligent Degree Planner presents years, terms, sessions, courses, and placeholders on a drag-and-drop planning canvas. The requirements panel shows required, optional, and unplanned work based on the learner’s Learner Program and Learner Program Requirements.\n- **Person Accounts** [Student Records]: Person Accounts represent individual students, staff, applicants, alumni, and other constituents in Education Cloud. In Education Cloud, data about individual people is stored on the Account object as Person Accounts. Person Accounts combine account and contact information into a single record and model faculty, staff, administrators, students, and alumni. Constituent Role records can track the current and past roles that a person has with the institution over time.\n- **Contact Points** [Student Records]: Contact Points store the addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses associated with a person in Education Cloud. The Student Management Account Record Page can include related lists for Contact Point Addresses, Contact Point Phones, and Contact Point Emails. Adding these related lists to the Person Account page layout gives staff access to a learner's additional contact information from the student record. This information appears alongside personal details, emergency contacts, citizenship information, demographics, and enrollment data.\n- **Relationships & Household** [Student Records]: Relationships & Household models how learners connect to family members, households, organizations, services, and other individuals. Person Accounts represent nearly all individuals, while household accounts represent families and business accounts represent organizations, schools, colleges, departments, and services. Group Membership objects link individuals to households, other individuals, services, or organizations. Education Cloud requires contacts to be related to multiple accounts and Group Membership to be enabled for household modeling.\n- **Education History** [Student Records]: Education History captures a person's prior education and supports admissions, alumni engagement, and transfer credit processes in Education Cloud. Admissions applications collect secondary school, college, or university education information from applicants. Education Cloud can prefill previously stored education information in later applications, while individual application task item records let applicants control which information is included for review. Admissions staff can verify education records and set their verification status during application processing.\n- **Examinations & Test Scores** [Student Records]: Examinations & Test Scores records standardized examinations and the scores supplied by applicants or students. Institutions create Examination records for standardized tests such as SAT, ACT, and Other. Applicants then provide test names, dates, and scores through the Test Results stage of an application. A Person Examination record represents the composite score or a single-section score associated with the applicant.\n- **Demographics & Languages** [Student Records]: Demographics & Languages brings biographic and demographic details together with a person's language information in Education Cloud. The Contact Profile object is associated with Contact and tracks biographic and demographic information. Education Cloud can combine data from Person Accounts and related Contact Profile records into a unified student record. Student Management presents citizenship and demographic information in the Learner Profile view alongside personal and contact details.\n- **Competency Tracking** [Student Success]: Competency Tracking in Education Cloud records the skills and proficiencies associated with a learner. Person Competencies give advisors and learners a structured way to document competencies acquired through coursework, cocurricular and extracurricular activities, internships, community service, and other experiences. When creating a Person Competency, users select the person’s proficiency level, verification status, effective dates, Competency, and stage. The person associated with the competency can be either an account or a contact. Students can also add competencies as interested or acquired through the learner portal.\n- **Traits & Affinities** [Student Success]: Traits & Affinities in Education Cloud captures student characteristics, preferences, and interests for personalized guidance. Person Traits reflect a student’s current profile, including strengths and opportunities. When creating a Person Trait, users enter a name, select Type and Category, associate a contact, and select the information Source. Administrators define the applicable Type, Category, and Source picklist values because these picklists have no values by default. Advisors can use traits with other student profile information to personalize guidance.\n- **Academic Advising** [Student Success]: Academic Advising in Education Cloud uses cases as advising workspaces where advisors coordinate success teams, care plans, tasks, alerts, and appointments. Institutions configure advising by enabling Student Success, creating a support process, and defining advising Case record types. Case record types represent distinct advising workspaces such as academic, career, or wellbeing support. Case team roles define staff responsibilities and access, while predefined case teams group staff into reusable teams. Success Team records enable advising workspaces and can connect predefined case teams as support pools.\n- **Academic Alerts** [Student Success]: Academic Alerts in Education Cloud use Record Alerts related to Case records to highlight learner performance or record changes that require action. Record Alerts help advisors monitor grades, attendance, behavior, courses, and other conditions that can impede learner success. Institutions can configure alert categories and severity levels, create alerts manually in the Student Success app, or use flows to automate and mass-apply alerts. The EDU Cloud: Create Grade Alert flow launches when a Course Offering Participant Result is updated and creates an alert when a grade falls below a C. If the learner has a failing grade, the flow also creates a task for a case team member.\n- **Career Development** [Student Success]: Career Development in Education Cloud combines career and life goals, career and interest assessments, involvement groups, competencies, traits, and affinities. Career Planning and Life Goals provides tools for students to define and manage goals beyond academics. Goal assignments can include a custom goal name, description, category, status, effective dates, target completion date, and related tasks. Students can track goals and tasks in the portal, while advisors can create personalized goals from a student’s case record. Action plans and campus resources help turn broad objectives into manageable steps.\n- **Fee Management** [Student Financials]: Fee Management in Education Cloud provides the framework for modeling tuition, application fees, course fees, meal plans, and other student charges. Products model student fees such as in-state tuition, international student tuition, lab fees, and meal plans. Institutions group these products into price books and associate the price books with academic intervals. This structure supports fees that change over time or vary according to learner attributes.\n- **Price Books & Schedules** [Student Financials]: Price Books & Schedules in Education Cloud organize fee products and associate their prices with academic periods. Administrators create products for student fees and add them to price books. Price books can represent different fee structures, such as fees for a particular year or learner population. A Price Book Academic Interval record associates a price book with an academic session, academic term, or academic year.\n- **Academic Orders** [Student Financials]: Academic Orders in Education Cloud connect student Orders to academic intervals. An institution can create an Order manually from the Orders page and add Order Products for the applicable fee products. The Order identifies the account and billing contact, while each Order Product specifies a product, quantity, and unit price. To link the Order to an academic interval, staff create a separate Academic Order, select the academic interval, and select the Order.\n- **Payment Processing** [Student Financials]: Payment Processing in Education Cloud lets students review invoices, initiate payments, and track their financial activity through the learner portal. The learner portal can include Financial Status at a Glance, Accounts Overview, Academic Year Charges, and Billing and Payments experiences. Financial Status at a Glance shows total charges, adjustments, balance due, next due date, and payment status. Students can also access invoices, running balances, and payment histories in one place.\n- **Financial Aid** [Student Financials]: Financial Aid in Education Cloud brings external financial aid management data into Data 360 and presents it to prospects, students, and staff. The financial aid data model represents the lifecycle from application through disbursement. Learner Financial Aid Application DMO represents applications such as FAFSA, scholarship, or grant applications, while Benefit Assignment DMO represents award offers and tracks offered amounts, accepted amounts, and statuses. Benefit Disbursement Period DMO defines payment periods, and Benefit Disbursement DMO and Benefit Disbursement Hold DMO represent payments and blocks on those payments.\n- **Program Design** [Mentoring]: Program Design defines structured mentoring support programs and the benefits they provide in Education Cloud. Education Cloud uses Program and Benefit Management to model mentoring programs and their related benefits. Facilitators create a support program, assign the Mentoring benefit type, add one or more benefits, and optionally add an intake assessment. Benefits can include a unit of measure, enrollment count, availability dates, minimum and maximum amounts, and a goal definition. Programs and benefits can be made available to participants through an Experience Cloud site.\n- **Mentor & Mentee Profiles** [Mentoring]: Mentor & Mentee Profiles capture the roles, preferences, interests, and other comparison information used for mentoring participants in Education Cloud. A Mentoring Profile helps facilitators connect a mentee with a suitable mentor. The profile identifies the participant's contact and the Program Enrollment or Provider Offering associated with that contact. A participant becomes a mentee through enrollment in a mentoring program, while mentors are represented as providers through Provider Offering records. These records link mentoring participation to the institution's person records.\n- **Mentor Matching** [Mentoring]: Mentor Matching helps facilitators assess potential mentors and connect them with mentees in Education Cloud. Mentor and mentee assessments collect information that supports matching. Separate assessments can capture what a mentee needs and what a mentor can provide, with submitted responses saved in records associated with the relevant enrollment. Facilitators can review these responses and populate Mentoring Profiles with comparison criteria. This process supports matches based on shared interests, needs, goals, skills, availability, or other configured profile fields.\n- **Benefit Assignment & Tracking** [Mentoring]: Benefit Assignment & Tracking records the mentoring benefit assigned when a mentee enrolls in a mentoring program. When a mentee enrolls in a mentoring program, Education Cloud creates a Program Enrollment and a Benefit Assignment for the mentoring benefit. Facilitators can review unmatched assignments from the Mentoring app home page and assign a mentor from the Benefit Assignment record. To make the manual match, a facilitator specifies the contact value for the Provider field. The assignment therefore connects the enrolled mentee with the contact who provides the mentoring benefit.\n- **Alumni Engagement** [Alumni & Advancement]: Alumni Engagement centralizes alumni information and activity in Education Cloud while preserving each person’s relationship with the institution over time. Education Cloud models alumni as Person Accounts and uses Constituent Role records to track their association with the institution over time. The Alumni Relations app lets coordinators and managers review alumni information, activities, alerts, cases, education, employment, relationships, and portal profile preferences in a centralized location. Staff can also use Timeline, Life Events, Interest Tags, Action Launcher, and KPI reports to understand involvement and identify engagement opportunities.\n- **Gift Management** [Alumni & Advancement]: Gift Management in Education Cloud supports gift entry, transaction processing, designations, soft credits, refunds, acknowledgments, and donor reporting. Fundraising provides guided processes for entering gifts individually or through gift batches. Processing a gift entry creates or updates related Gift Transaction, Gift Transaction Designation, and Gift Soft Credit records, and batch dry runs validate data before records are committed. Gift Entry Grid templates can be configured to capture the fields and processes required for different fundraising scenarios.\n- **Pledges & Commitments** [Alumni & Advancement]: Pledges & Commitments in Education Cloud uses Gift Commitment records to manage one-time pledges, multipayment gifts, recurring gifts, and in-kind commitments. A Gift Commitment records a donor’s promised gift and can represent monetary or in-kind support. Staff select a Recurring schedule type for donations paid on an ongoing schedule or a Custom schedule type for nonrecurring gifts paid in one or more installments. Commitments can also be connected to campaigns, opportunities, Gift Default Designations, and soft credits.\n- **Volunteer Management** [Alumni & Advancement]: Volunteer Management in Education Cloud supports volunteer programs, opportunities, applications, scheduling, contributed hours, and impact reporting. Volunteer Management streamlines program administration and the volunteer experience for programs such as alumni mentoring, parent associations, and campus events. Institutions can publish volunteer opportunities on an Experience Cloud site, collect and evaluate applications, schedule volunteers, and track volunteer hours. Reporting then helps staff measure volunteer impact.\n- **Fundraising Campaigns** [Alumni & Advancement]: Fundraising Campaigns in Education Cloud organize donor outreach and connect campaign activity with commitments, transactions, and reporting. Education Cloud uses Salesforce campaigns for fundraising outreach and augments them with Outreach Source Codes. Institutions can create one or more source codes for a campaign to distinguish channels, messages, audiences, and outreach methods such as mailings, digital advertisements, or events. A source code can be associated with gift entry and gift transactions so staff can identify which outreach generated a donation.\n- **Appointment Scheduling** [Appointments]: Appointment Scheduling provides centralized appointment booking for learners and staff in Education Cloud. Education Cloud uses Salesforce Scheduler and the EDUScheduler Omniscript to provide appointment scheduling. Learners access scheduling through an Experience Cloud site, while staff use the Staff Appointment Scheduling component on the learner's person account record. A learner must have an existing related case before the learner or a staff member can schedule an appointment. The My Appointments Header component provides a workflow in which learners select a success team, advisor, topic or subtopic, and then check availability before choosing a time slot.\n- **Staff Availability** [Appointments]: Staff Availability determines when and where service resources can accept appointments in Education Cloud. Education Cloud uses shifts from a staff member's service territory to determine available appointment times. Although operating hours are required when creating service territories, Salesforce Scheduler for Education Cloud uses shifts rather than operating hours to calculate staff availability. A shift can cover one or more service territories, appointment topics, and engagement channels. This structure supports patterns such as on-campus advising on selected days and virtual appointments on other days.\n- **Waitlist & Walk-ins** [Appointments]: Waitlist & Walk-ins supports in-person drop-in consultations through waitlists associated with available service resources. Drop In Appointments provides lobby management for learners who arrive for an in-person consultation without a scheduled meeting. Front-office staff create a waitlist for available service resources and generate a QR code that links to a waitlist check-in page. Learners scan the code, select a topic, and add themselves to the waitlist. The related shift must include an in-person engagement channel and drop-in appointment topics for drop-in availability to appear.\n- **Virtual Appointments** [Appointments]: Virtual Appointments enables learners and staff to meet through audio and video from Education Cloud appointment experiences. To use Virtual Calls, administrators configure Salesforce Scheduler, enable Video Calls, connect Amazon to Salesforce, and deploy the required AWS services. They create a named credential for the AWS Chime callout endpoint and add a dedicated Virtual Calls page to the Experience Cloud site. Appointment data must also include the video type before video appointments are configured. The Video Call component can be added to the app utility bar so that staff can join calls easily.\n- **Director Insights** [Education Analytics]: Director Insights is the Student Success dashboard for directors in Education Cloud. The Director Insights dashboard helps Student Success directors monitor institutional-level performance annually. Its Institutional Overview page shows metrics such as total students, students enrolled in advisor services, the advisor-to-student ratio, average cumulative grade point average, and changes from a previous period. It also presents multiyear institutional performance based on enrollment, total students, and average cumulative grade point average.\n- **Recruitment Analytics** [Education Analytics]: Recruitment Analytics provides CRM Analytics dashboards for recruitment and admissions roles in Education Cloud. The Recruitment Insights dashboard helps recruiters analyze pipeline health and the status of requests for information. It shows the count of applications in each pipeline stage, the overall count and status distribution of RFIs, and the number of applications generated by each lead source. Recruiters can use these insights to prioritize prospects and schedule appointments effectively.\n- **Student Success Metrics** [Education Analytics]: Student Success Metrics uses Education Cloud analytics to monitor learner support, academic progress, advisor activity, and program performance. The Advisor Insights dashboard contains Alerts, Care Plans, and Appointments pages. Advisors can review caseload and alert counts, active alerts, average case resolution time, care plans that are overdue or on track, appointment totals, and appointment utilization and cancellation rates. Charts also distribute alerts, care plans, and appointments by criteria such as case record type, status, channel, and topic type.\n- **Student Recruitment Agent** [Agentforce]: The Student Recruitment Agent in Education Cloud helps prospective students and families learn about an institution, its programs, admissions processes, and campus offerings. The Student Recruitment Agent includes subagents for Admissions and Enrollments FAQ, Admissions Application, Campus Tours, Visits, and Events Registration, and Request for Information. These subagents use actions such as Answer Questions with Knowledge, Get Learning Program Data, Create Admissions Application, and Create Academic Interest. They support recruitment outreach automation, admissions and application-status questions, application initiation, and requests for recruiter follow-up. An escalation subagent can redirect unanswered conversations to a human representative and can include the Create Inquiry action.\n- **Student Goals Agent** [Agentforce]: The Student Goals Agent recommends campus resources to learners, including courses, support programs, and involvement groups, based on learner information and assessment insights. Student Advising Topics in Agentforce includes the Student Advising and Campus Engagement subagent with Summarize Advising Case, Find Advising Cases, and Find Campus Resources actions. Advisors can use it to understand advising cases, answer student questions about policies and campus resources, and identify next-best actions. Knowledge articles ground policy answers, while an Einstein search retriever finds campus resources from configured data streams. Institutions must apply access controls that comply with their policies and data privacy requirements.\n- **Student Financials Agent** [Agentforce]: The Student Financials Agent in Education Cloud helps students retrieve account balances and understand their fees. The Student Financials Agent uses Get Account Balance, Query Records, Get Record Details, Get Fee Details, and Get Fee Breakdown actions. To retrieve fee information, it queries Academic Term, Academic Term Enrollment, Academic Order, Order Product, Billing Schedule, Credit Memo, Invoice, and Invoice Line records. Administrators configure the EDU Student Financials: Get Fee Details for Academic Order flow and grant students the required Education Cloud, Student Financials, pricing, flow, and Experience Cloud permissions. Sharing settings and sharing sets control access to account and academic term enrollment records.\n- **Donor Engagement Agent** [Agentforce]: The Donor Engagement Agent in Education Cloud helps major giving officers preserve donor intelligence and automate advancement follow-up. The Donor Engagement Agent template includes Donor Meeting Notes, Donor Task Management, and Agent Router subagents. Donor Meeting Notes uses the Prepare Donor Meeting Notes prompt template and actions such as Identify Record by Name, Query Record, Create Interaction, and Create Interaction Summary. Donor Task Management uses actions including Create a To-Do, Extract Fields and Values From User Input, Update Record, and Get Record Details. Together, these capabilities support alumni and donor engagement automation by preserving meeting context and translating next steps into Salesforce records.\n- **Student Portal** [Experience Cloud]: The Student Portal provides enrolled learners with a secure, self-service Education Cloud experience for academic, support, appointment, and financial activities. The Education Portal template includes pages and components for common student activities. Its Learning Programs area lists the portal user’s learning program plans, while Success Team and Support Programs provide access to assigned resources and available services. Student success components also let learners view advisors, post questions, check availability, schedule appointments, manage action plans, and track career and life goals. Institutions add and configure these components with Experience Builder.\n- **Applicant Portal** [Experience Cloud]: The Applicant Portal supports Education Cloud recruitment and admissions self-service from registration and requests for information through application submission and tracking. The Education Portal template provides Home, Applications, Requests for Information, and Appointments areas for applicants. Home displays created applications, requests for information, tasks, and appointments, while Applications lists existing applications and lets users start a new one. Applicants can raise and monitor requests for information and schedule appointments with institution staff. When an applicant registers through the learner portal, Education Cloud automatically creates a person account for that individual.\n- **Alumni Portal** [Experience Cloud]: The Alumni Portal gives former learners a secure Education Cloud experience for maintaining profiles and connecting with the institution and other alumni. The Education Portal template includes components designed for alumni alongside those for applicants and students. The Alumni Profile area lets portal users populate personal information to create their profiles, and the Directory provides a searchable list of public profiles. Alumni can therefore maintain a presence in the community and find other alumni from the institution. A single portal login can also serve alumni who are applying for continued learning or otherwise hold overlapping constituent roles.\n- **Mobile Experience** [Experience Cloud]: Mobile Experience extends the Education Cloud learner portal to supported mobile devices so learners can access key self-service functions while away from a desktop. Education Cloud student success components bring learner self-service functions into an Experience Cloud portal. On supported mobile devices, learners can view details of their care plans and assigned success teams and schedule appointments with advisors. The broader component set also supports past and upcoming appointments, advisor information, support programs, assessments, benefits, tasks, action plans, and goals. Available functions depend on the components that the institution adds and configures.\n- **Data Consolidation** [Data 360]: Data Consolidation brings disparate learner and education data into a central Data 360 environment for Education Cloud. Education Cloud uses Data 360 to connect and prepare large volumes of data from institutional systems. Student information systems can provide profiles, registrations, payments, attendance, and grades, while learning management systems can provide usage, interactions, and assessments. Enterprise resource planning systems contribute financial records, tuition and fee details, financial aid, billing, and holds. Institutions can also ingest data from systems for housing, dining, event registration, and other campus services.\n- **Identity Resolution** [Data 360]: Identity Resolution links student person records from multiple data sources into unified profiles. The Education data kit includes an identity resolution ruleset with match and reconciliation rules for linking multiple sources of data into a unified profile. Implementations can also create a ruleset for the data platform after ingesting and mapping source records. For financial aid, the ruleset is created on the Individual object to match records from the financial aid system with Salesforce Person Accounts. After the ruleset is published and run, administrators can confirm that it produces a nonzero data consolidation rate.\n- **Unified Records** [Data 360]: Unified Records represent consolidated learner identities created by linking related source records. Data 360 harmonizes imported institutional data into a unified learner profile. This profile gives institutions a central view for understanding and supporting current learners, engaging alumni, and preparing for future learners. Unified data can support reliable analytics and predictions, scaled automation, trusted AI grounding, and customized learner experiences. Identity resolution supplies the links between source records and the resulting unified profile.\n- **Data Mapping & Standards** [Data 360]: Data Mapping & Standards aligns fields and objects from connected institutional sources with Data 360 data model objects. After importing education data, administrators map it to the required Data 360 DMOs. Connecting a source allows Data 360 to access its objects and fields so that mapping sets can be created between the source and the Data 360 model. Education Cloud provides education-specific DMOs for data from learning management, student information, engagement, advancement, and enterprise resource planning systems. Harmonized DMO data can then be used to build segments, create calculated insights, and analyze key metrics.\n\n## Key Objects (54)\n### Recruitment\n- **Lead**: Lead represents a suspect who has not yet indicated interest in the institution.\n- **ContactProfile**: ContactProfile is associated with a Contact and stores biographic and demographic information used with the person's record.\n- **Campaign**: Campaign represents an outreach effort, including recruitment-event campaigns used by the Student Recruitment Agent.\n- **CampaignMember**: CampaignMember associates a Campaign with either a Lead or a Contact and records the member's Status.\n### Admissions\n- **IndividualApplication**: IndividualApplication represents a person's admissions application to an institution or program and can be related to the account of the person submitting it or the contact preparing it on the applicant's behalf.\n- **Applicant**: Applicant identifies an applicant associated with an account and an IndividualApplication record.\n- **DocumentChecklistItem**: DocumentChecklistItem represents a document or other checklist requirement associated with a parent record, such as an application.\n- **PreliminaryApplicationRef**: PreliminaryApplicationRef is the schema object for Preliminary Application Reference, which stores applications that are in progress so applicants can finish them later.\n### Academic Operations\n- **LearningProgram**: LearningProgram represents a Learning Program containing the curricula required for a learner to obtain a credential.\n- **LearningCourse**: LearningCourse stores detailed information about a specific Learning Course within a larger learning framework.\n- **CourseOffering**: CourseOffering represents a scheduled section or instance of a Learning Course.\n- **AcademicTerm**: AcademicTerm represents an academic period used for reporting, Course Offerings, registration, and enrollment.\n- **AcademicSession**: AcademicSession specifies Course Offering time periods based on an institution's calendar.\n- **LearningOutcomeItem**: LearningOutcomeItem represents the mapping between a learning and its related outcome.\n- **CourseCreditTransferAppln**: CourseCreditTransferAppln represents a Course Credit Transfer Application, which is a request to apply academic credit for courses taken at another institution to a current degree.\n- **LearningEquivalency**: LearningEquivalency stores the details of an equivalency between external and internal learnings.\n### Enrollment\n- **LearnerProgram**: LearnerProgram represents a learner’s enrollment in a learning program and links the learner’s AccountId to ProgramId.\n- **CourseOfferingParticipant**: CourseOfferingParticipant represents an account’s participation in a specific course offering.\n- **LearningAchievement**: LearningAchievement represents the outcome of a learning activity for an account.\n- **LearnerPathway**: LearnerPathway represents a learner’s planned path toward completion of an enrolled learning program.\n### Student Records\n- **Account**: Account stores individual people as Person Accounts in Education Cloud, combining account and contact information in one record.\n- **ContactPointAddress**: ContactPointAddress stores an address associated with a person and can appear in the Contact Point Addresses related list on a Person Account.\n- **ContactPointPhone**: ContactPointPhone stores a person's phone number as a contact point.\n- **ContactPointEmail**: ContactPointEmail stores a person's email address as a contact point.\n- **ContactContactRelation**: Contact Contact Relationship, whose API name is ContactContactRelation, represents a relationship between contacts, such as an applicant's connection to a guardian, sibling, or recommender.\n- **PersonEducation**: PersonEducation stores a person's education history and is used in application, alumni, and transfer-credit contexts.\n- **PersonExamination**: PersonExamination stores standardized examination results associated with a person and can represent composite or section-level scores.\n- **PersonLanguage**: PersonLanguage stores a person's language information and proficiency, including information collected through admissions applications.\n- **PersonEmployment**: PersonEmployment stores employment history for a person and can be displayed on alumni records and profiles.\n### Student Success\n- **PersonCompetency**: PersonCompetency represents a Competency associated with a person who can be either an account or a contact.\n- **PersonTrait**: PersonTrait represents a characteristic in a student’s current profile and is associated with a contact.\n- **PersonAffinity**: PersonAffinity represents a student’s past or current interest and is associated with a contact.\n- **CareCoordinator**: CareCoordinator records a named coordinator assignment for an Account, including the coordinator’s role and the assignment’s StartDate and EndDate.\n- **CareRole**: CareRole defines a named care role with a Description and an active status.\n- **Case**: Case represents a learner support request or advising workspace in Education Cloud.\n### Student Financials\n- **Product2**: Product2 represents a product used to model a student fee, such as tuition, a lab fee, or a meal plan.\n- **Pricebook2**: Pricebook2 represents a price book that groups products used in a student fee structure.\n- **PricebookEntry**: PricebookEntry connects a Product2 record to a Pricebook2 record and supplies the product’s UnitPrice in that price book.\n- **Order**: Order represents a learner’s product request and financial total; it is distinct from the Academic Order record that links it to an academic interval.\n- **OrderProduct**: OrderProduct represents a product line on an Order.\n### Mentoring\n- **Program**: Program represents a mentoring support program in Education Cloud.\n- **MentoringProfile**: MentoringProfile stores the mentoring role and configured comparison information for a participant, including Name, Role, Skills, Goals, and Availability.\n- **Benefit**: Benefit represents mentoring support made available through a Program.\n- **BenefitAssignment**: BenefitAssignment tracks the assignment of a Benefit between the related mentor and mentee profiles through BenefitId, MentorProfileId, and MenteeProfileId, with Name, Status, AssignedDate, and CompletedDate recording its lifecycle.\n### Alumni & Advancement\n- **GiftTransaction**: GiftTransaction records a received donation, whether it is a one-time gift or an installment associated with a commitment.\n- **GiftCommitment**: GiftCommitment represents a donor’s promised contribution, including recurring, multipayment, and other commitment structures.\n- **VolunteerActivity**: VolunteerActivity represents a volunteer’s participation and contributed effort for a dated activity.\n### Appointments\n- **ServiceAppointment**: ServiceAppointment represents an appointment scheduled for a learner and one or more service resources in Education Cloud.\n- **ServiceResource**: ServiceResource represents a staff member who can be made available for appointments, such as an academic advisor, admissions counselor, or financial aid advisor.\n- **ServiceTerritory**: ServiceTerritory represents a location or organizational grouping used to organize appointments and staff availability.\n### Data 360\n- **DataStream**: DataStream represents an ingestion stream that collects source data and stores it in Data 360 for Education Cloud.\n- **IdentityResolutionRuleset**: IdentityResolutionRuleset represents the match and reconciliation configuration used to link records from multiple sources into a unified learner profile.\n- **MatchRule**: MatchRule represents a matching condition within an identity resolution ruleset.\n- **UnifiedIndividual**: UnifiedIndividual represents a learner identity produced by linking related person records from multiple data sources.\n\n## Object Fields\n### Lead (6 fields)\n- FirstName (Text): Prospect's first name\n- LastName (Text): Prospect's last name\n- Email (Email): Primary email address for recruitment communications\n- LeadSource (Picklist): Channel through which the prospect first engaged (web inquiry, campus event, referral)\n- Status (Picklist): Current stage in the recruitment funnel (new, contacted, qualified, converted)\n- Company (Text): High school or current institution name\n### ContactProfile (4 fields)\n- CitizenshipStatus (Picklist): Citizenship classification (citizen, permanent resident, international)\n- ResidencyStatus (Picklist): In-state or out-of-state residency for tuition classification\n- PreferredName (Text): Name the student prefers to be called\n- ContactId (Lookup): Reference to the person account contact record\n### Campaign (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Campaign name\n- Type (Picklist): Campaign type (event, email, social media, direct mail)\n- Status (Picklist): Campaign lifecycle status (planned, active, completed)\n- StartDate (Date): Campaign start date\n- EndDate (Date): Campaign end date\n- NumberOfLeads (Number): Total leads generated by this campaign\n### CampaignMember (4 fields)\n- Status (Picklist): Response status within the campaign (sent, responded, attended, converted)\n- CampaignId (Lookup): Reference to the parent campaign\n- LeadId (Lookup): Reference to the lead record if the member is a prospect\n- ContactId (Lookup): Reference to the contact record if the member is an existing student\n### IndividualApplication (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated application identifier\n- ApplicationStatus (Picklist): Current status (draft, submitted, under review, decision rendered)\n- ApplicationType (Picklist): Type of application (freshman, transfer, graduate, international)\n- SubmittedDate (DateTime): Timestamp when the applicant submitted the application\n- DecisionStatus (Picklist): Admission decision (accepted, denied, waitlisted, conditional)\n- ProgramId (Lookup): The academic program the applicant is applying to\n### Applicant (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Applicant display name\n- AccountId (Lookup): Reference to the person account\n- IndividualApplicationId (Lookup): Reference to the application record\n- ApplicantType (Picklist): Applicant classification (first-time, transfer, returning)\n### DocumentChecklistItem (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Document type name (transcript, essay, recommendation letter)\n- Status (Picklist): Document status (required, received, verified, waived)\n- IsRequired (Checkbox): Whether this document is mandatory for application completion\n- ReceivedDate (Date): Date the document was received\n- ParentRecordId (Lookup): Reference to the parent application record\n### PreliminaryApplicationRef (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Applicant name\n- IntendedProgram (Text): Program the prospect is interested in\n- IntendedTerm (Text): Academic term of intended enrollment\n- Status (Picklist): Preliminary application status\n- AccountId (Lookup): Reference to the person account\n### LearningProgram (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Program title (e.g., Bachelor of Science in Computer Science)\n- ProgramType (Picklist): Degree type (bachelor, master, doctoral, certificate)\n- TotalCreditsRequired (Number): Minimum credits needed for completion\n- MinimumGPA (Number): Minimum cumulative GPA required for good standing\n- DepartmentId (Lookup): Academic department that owns this program\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether the program is actively accepting enrollments\n### LearningCourse (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Course title\n- CourseCode (Text): Subject area and course number (e.g., CS 101)\n- CreditValue (Number): Number of credits awarded upon completion\n- Description (LongTextArea): Course description for the catalog\n- Level (Picklist): Course level (introductory, intermediate, advanced, graduate)\n### CourseOffering (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Offering display name including section number\n- SectionNumber (Text): Section identifier within the term\n- Capacity (Number): Maximum enrollment capacity\n- DeliveryMode (Picklist): Instruction mode (in-person, online, hybrid)\n- CourseId (Lookup): Reference to the parent course definition\n- AcademicTermId (Lookup): The academic term this offering is scheduled in\n### AcademicTerm (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Term name (e.g., Fall 2026, Spring 2027)\n- StartDate (Date): First day of the term\n- EndDate (Date): Last day of the term\n- RegistrationStartDate (Date): When course registration opens\n- RegistrationEndDate (Date): Registration deadline\n- Type (Picklist): Term type (fall, spring, summer, winter)\n### AcademicSession (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Time period name (e.g., Summer Session A)\n- StartDate (Date): Period start date\n- EndDate (Date): Period end date\n- AcademicTermId (Lookup): Parent academic term\n### LearningOutcomeItem (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Objective title\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed statement of what the student will achieve\n- AssessmentMethod (Picklist): How achievement is measured (exam, project, portfolio)\n- CourseId (Lookup): Course this objective belongs to\n### CourseCreditTransferAppln (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Transfer credit display name\n- OriginatingInstitution (Text): Name of the institution where credits were earned\n- OriginalCourseName (Text): Course title at the originating institution\n- CreditValue (Number): Number of credits transferred\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### LearningEquivalency (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Equivalency rule name\n- ExternalCourseName (Text): Course name at the external institution\n- ExternalInstitution (Text): Institution the equivalency applies to\n- InternalCourseId (Lookup): The equivalent internal course\n- CreditValue (Number): Credits awarded for the equivalency\n### LearnerProgram (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): System-generated enrollment identifier\n- EnrollmentStatus (Picklist): Current status (active, on hold, leave, withdrawn, graduated)\n- StartDate (Date): Enrollment start date\n- ExpectedGraduationDate (Date): Projected graduation date\n- CumulativeGPA (Number): Running cumulative grade point average\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n- ProgramId (Lookup): Academic program the student is enrolled in\n### CourseOfferingParticipant (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Participant display name\n- ParticipantRole (Picklist): Role in the offering (student, TA, auditor)\n- RegistrationStatus (Picklist): Status (registered, waitlisted, dropped, completed)\n- CreditMode (Picklist): Credit type (credit, audit, pass-fail)\n- CourseOfferingId (Lookup): The course offering this participant is registered in\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### LearningAchievement (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Achievement display name\n- GradeValue (Text): Letter grade or pass/fail designation\n- CreditsEarned (Number): Credits awarded for this achievement\n- CreditsAttempted (Number): Credits attempted (may differ for withdrawals)\n- QualityPoints (Number): Grade points used in GPA calculation\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### LearnerPathway (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Plan display name\n- Status (Picklist): Plan status (draft, active, completed)\n- TargetCompletionDate (Date): Projected completion date\n- TotalPlannedCredits (Number): Total credits mapped in the plan\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### Account (6 fields)\n- FirstName (Text): Student's first name\n- LastName (Text): Student's last name\n- PersonBirthdate (Date): Date of birth\n- StudentId__c (Text): Institutional student identifier\n- PersonEmail (Email): Primary email address\n- RecordTypeId (Lookup): Person account record type\n### ContactPointAddress (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Address label (home, school, billing)\n- Street (TextArea): Street address lines\n- City (Text): City name\n- StateCode (Text): State or province\n- PostalCode (Text): ZIP or postal code\n- Country (Text): Country name\n- UsageType (Picklist): Address purpose (home, school, billing, permanent)\n### ContactPointPhone (4 fields)\n- TelephoneNumber (Phone): Phone number\n- UsageType (Picklist): Phone usage type (mobile, home, work)\n- IsPrimary (Checkbox): Whether this is the primary phone number\n- ParentId (Lookup): Reference to the person account\n### ContactPointEmail (4 fields)\n- EmailAddress (Email): Email address\n- UsageType (Picklist): Email usage type (personal, institutional, work)\n- IsPrimary (Checkbox): Whether this is the primary email\n- ParentId (Lookup): Reference to the person account\n### ContactContactRelation (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Relationship display name\n- Type (Picklist): Relationship type (parent, guardian, sibling, spouse, emergency)\n- IsPrimaryContact (Checkbox): Whether this is the primary family contact\n- ContactId (Lookup): The student in the relationship\n- RelatedContactId (Lookup): The related person\n### PersonEducation (6 fields)\n- InstitutionName (Text): Name of the school or institution\n- DegreeEarned (Text): Degree or diploma earned\n- GPA (Number): Grade point average at the institution\n- StartDate (Date): Attendance start date\n- EndDate (Date): Attendance end date\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### PersonExamination (5 fields)\n- ExamName (Text): Name of the examination (SAT, ACT, GRE)\n- ExamDate (Date): Date the exam was taken\n- TotalScore (Number): Composite or total score\n- SectionScores (LongTextArea): Individual section scores\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### PersonLanguage (4 fields)\n- Language (Picklist): Language name\n- ProficiencyLevel (Picklist): Overall proficiency (beginner, intermediate, advanced, native)\n- IsPrimary (Checkbox): Whether this is the student's primary language\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### PersonEmployment (6 fields)\n- EmployerName (Text): Name of the employer\n- JobTitle (Text): Position title\n- StartDate (Date): Employment start date\n- EndDate (Date): Employment end date (null if current)\n- EmploymentType (Picklist): Type (full-time, part-time, internship, work-study)\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### PersonCompetency (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Competency name\n- ProficiencyLevel (Picklist): Current proficiency (developing, proficient, advanced, expert)\n- AssessmentDate (Date): Date the competency was last assessed\n- AssessmentMethod (Picklist): How proficiency was measured (exam, project, observation)\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### PersonTrait (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Trait name\n- Category (Picklist): Trait category (academic, interpersonal, leadership)\n- Strength (Picklist): Strength level (emerging, developing, strong)\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### PersonAffinity (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Affinity name\n- Type (Picklist): Affinity type (academic interest, career aspiration, extracurricular)\n- InterestLevel (Picklist): Degree of interest (casual, moderate, strong)\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### CareCoordinator (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Coordinator assignment name\n- Role (Picklist): Support role (academic advisor, career counselor, student affairs)\n- StartDate (Date): Assignment start date\n- EndDate (Date): Assignment end date (null if current)\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student being supported\n- CoordinatorId (Lookup): Staff member providing support\n### CareRole (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Role title\n- Description (TextArea): Role scope and responsibilities\n- MaxCaseload (Number): Maximum number of students per coordinator\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this role is currently available for assignment\n### Case (6 fields)\n- Subject (Text): Alert or case subject\n- Status (Picklist): Case status (new, in progress, resolved, closed)\n- Priority (Picklist): Urgency level (low, medium, high, critical)\n- Type (Picklist): Case type (academic alert, conduct, financial hold, general)\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n- Description (LongTextArea): Detailed description of the alert or issue\n### Product2 (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Fee product name (e.g., In-State Tuition Per Credit)\n- ProductCode (Text): Unique fee code\n- Family (Picklist): Fee family (tuition, fees, housing, meal plans)\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this fee is currently applicable\n- Description (TextArea): Fee description\n### Pricebook2 (3 fields)\n- Name (Text): Price book name (e.g., Fall 2026 Undergraduate)\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this price book is active\n- Description (TextArea): Price book description and applicability\n### PricebookEntry (4 fields)\n- UnitPrice (Currency): Fee amount\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this entry is active\n- Product2Id (Lookup): Reference to the fee product\n- Pricebook2Id (Lookup): Reference to the price book\n### Order (6 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): Order identifier\n- Status (Picklist): Order status (draft, activated, paid, past due)\n- TotalAmount (Currency): Total charges on the order\n- TermId (Lookup): Academic term this order covers\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n- EffectiveDate (Date): Date the order becomes effective\n### OrderProduct (5 fields)\n- Quantity (Number): Number of units (e.g., credit hours)\n- UnitPrice (Currency): Price per unit\n- TotalPrice (Currency): Line item total (quantity x unit price)\n- OrderId (Lookup): Parent academic order\n- Product2Id (Lookup): Fee product being charged\n### Program (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Program name\n- ProgramType (Picklist): Type of mentoring (peer, career, academic, alumni)\n- Status (Picklist): Program status (planned, active, completed)\n- StartDate (Date): Program start date\n- EndDate (Date): Program end date\n- MaxParticipants (Number): Maximum mentee capacity\n### MentoringProfile (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Profile display name\n- Role (Picklist): Participant role (mentor, mentee)\n- Skills (LongTextArea): Skills and areas of expertise\n- Goals (LongTextArea): Mentoring goals and expectations\n- Availability (Picklist): Availability level (weekly, biweekly, monthly)\n- ProgramId (Lookup): Mentoring program this profile belongs to\n- AccountId (Lookup): Person account of the participant\n### Benefit (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Benefit name\n- Type (Picklist): Benefit type (tutoring, coaching, networking, workshop)\n- Description (TextArea): What this benefit provides\n- Frequency (Picklist): Expected delivery frequency (weekly, monthly, as needed)\n- ProgramId (Lookup): Program this benefit belongs to\n### BenefitAssignment (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Assignment display name\n- Status (Picklist): Assignment status (assigned, in progress, completed)\n- AssignedDate (Date): Date the benefit was assigned\n- CompletedDate (Date): Date the benefit was delivered\n- BenefitId (Lookup): Reference to the mentoring benefit\n- MentorProfileId (Lookup): Reference to the mentor's profile\n- MenteeProfileId (Lookup): Reference to the mentee's profile\n### GiftTransaction (7 fields)\n- Name (AutoNumber): Transaction identifier\n- Amount (Currency): Gift amount\n- GiftDate (Date): Date the gift was made\n- Designation (Picklist): Fund designation (unrestricted, scholarship, building, endowment)\n- PaymentMethod (Picklist): How the gift was made (check, credit card, wire, securities)\n- AcknowledgmentStatus (Picklist): Status of the tax acknowledgment letter\n- DonorId (Lookup): Person account of the donor\n### GiftCommitment (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Commitment display name\n- TotalAmount (Currency): Total pledged amount\n- AmountReceived (Currency): Amount paid to date\n- PaymentSchedule (Picklist): Payment frequency (monthly, quarterly, annually)\n- StartDate (Date): Pledge start date\n- EndDate (Date): Pledge end date\n- DonorId (Lookup): Person account of the pledging donor\n### VolunteerActivity (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Activity name\n- ActivityType (Picklist): Type of volunteer activity (mentoring, board service, event, career panel)\n- HoursContributed (Number): Hours spent on the activity\n- ActivityDate (Date): Date of the activity\n- VolunteerId (Lookup): Person account of the volunteer\n### ServiceAppointment (7 fields)\n- Subject (Text): Appointment subject or topic\n- Status (Picklist): Appointment status (scheduled, checked in, completed, no show)\n- SchedStartTime (DateTime): Scheduled start time\n- SchedEndTime (DateTime): Scheduled end time\n- AppointmentType (Picklist): Meeting type (advising, tutoring, financial aid, administrative)\n- ServiceTerritoryId (Lookup): Department or location for the appointment\n- AccountId (Lookup): Student person account\n### ServiceResource (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Staff member name\n- ResourceType (Picklist): Resource type (advisor, tutor, counselor, administrator)\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this resource is currently available\n- RelatedRecordId (Lookup): Reference to the staff user record\n### ServiceTerritory (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Territory name (e.g., Academic Advising Center)\n- Description (TextArea): Territory description and services offered\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Whether this territory is currently active\n- OperatingHoursId (Lookup): Operating hours configuration\n### DataStream (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Data stream name\n- SourceSystem (Text): Name of the external source system\n- Status (Picklist): Stream status (active, paused, error)\n- Schedule (Text): Extraction schedule (daily, hourly, real-time)\n- LastRunDate (DateTime): Timestamp of the last successful extraction\n### IdentityResolutionRuleset (4 fields)\n- Name (Text): Ruleset name\n- Status (Picklist): Ruleset status (active, draft, disabled)\n- AutoMergeThreshold (Percent): Confidence score above which records merge automatically\n- ReviewThreshold (Percent): Confidence score above which records are flagged for manual review\n### MatchRule (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Rule name\n- MatchField (Text): The field being compared (email, name, date of birth)\n- MatchType (Picklist): Comparison method (exact, fuzzy, phonetic)\n- Weight (Number): Importance weight for scoring (1-100)\n- RulesetId (Lookup): Parent identity resolution ruleset\n### UnifiedIndividual (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Unified record display name\n- SourceCount (Number): Number of source records that were merged\n- ConfidenceScore (Percent): Overall confidence in the identity match\n- LastResolvedDate (DateTime): Timestamp of the last resolution run\n- AccountId (Lookup): Reference to the resulting person account\n\n## Object Relationships\n- Lead -> Campaign (lookup): Leads are associated with recruitment campaigns through campaign members\n- ContactProfile -> Account (child): Each contact profile belongs to a person account\n- Campaign -> CampaignMember (parent): Campaigns contain campaign member records for each participant\n- CampaignMember -> Campaign (child): Each member belongs to a campaign\n- CampaignMember -> Lead (lookup): Member may reference a lead record\n- IndividualApplication -> Applicant (parent): Each application has an applicant record linking to the person\n- IndividualApplication -> LearningProgram (lookup): Applications reference the target academic program\n- IndividualApplication -> DocumentChecklistItem (parent): Applications have required document checklist items\n- Applicant -> Account (lookup): Each applicant references a person account\n- Applicant -> IndividualApplication (child): Each applicant is linked to an application\n- DocumentChecklistItem -> IndividualApplication (child): Checklist items belong to an application\n- PreliminaryApplicationRef -> Account (lookup): Preliminary applications link to person accounts\n- PreliminaryApplicationRef -> LearningProgram (lookup): References the intended academic program\n- LearningProgram -> LearnerProgram (parent): Programs have enrolled students through program enrollment records\n- LearningProgram -> LearningCourse (parent): Programs define required and elective courses\n- LearningCourse -> CourseOffering (parent): Courses have multiple offerings across terms\n- LearningCourse -> LearningProgram (lookup): Courses belong to academic programs\n- CourseOffering -> LearningCourse (child): Each offering belongs to a course\n- CourseOffering -> AcademicTerm (lookup): Offerings are scheduled within academic terms\n- CourseOffering -> CourseOfferingParticipant (parent): Offerings have registered participants\n- AcademicTerm -> CourseOffering (parent): Terms contain course offerings\n- AcademicTerm -> AcademicSession (parent): Terms can be subdivided into time periods\n- AcademicSession -> AcademicTerm (child): Each time period belongs to an academic term\n- LearningOutcomeItem -> LearningCourse (child): Objectives are defined within courses\n- LearningOutcomeItem -> PersonCompetency (lookup): Objectives map to competency assessments\n- CourseCreditTransferAppln -> Account (child): Transfer credits belong to a student\n- CourseCreditTransferAppln -> LearningEquivalency (lookup): Transfer credits reference equivalency rules for mapping\n- LearningEquivalency -> LearningCourse (lookup): Equivalencies map to internal course definitions\n- LearnerProgram -> Account (child): Each enrollment belongs to a student\n- LearnerProgram -> LearningProgram (lookup): Enrollment references the academic program\n- CourseOfferingParticipant -> CourseOffering (child): Participants belong to a course offering\n- CourseOfferingParticipant -> Account (lookup): Participants reference the student person account\n- LearningAchievement -> Account (child): Achievements belong to a student\n- LearningAchievement -> CourseOffering (lookup): Achievements reference the completed course offering\n- LearnerPathway -> Account (child): Learning plans belong to a student\n- LearnerPathway -> LearnerProgram (lookup): Plans are tied to a specific program enrollment\n- Account -> LearnerProgram (parent): Students have program enrollments\n- Account -> ContactContactRelation (parent): Students have relationship records to family members\n- Account -> PersonEducation (parent): Students have education history records\n- ContactPointAddress -> Account (child): Addresses belong to a person account\n- ContactPointPhone -> Account (child): Phone records belong to a person account\n- ContactPointEmail -> Account (child): Email records belong to a person account\n- ContactContactRelation -> Account (child): Relationships link two person accounts\n- PersonEducation -> Account (child): Education history belongs to a student\n- PersonExamination -> Account (child): Examination records belong to a student\n- PersonLanguage -> Account (child): Language records belong to a student\n- PersonEmployment -> Account (child): Employment records belong to a student\n- PersonCompetency -> Account (child): Competencies belong to a student\n- PersonCompetency -> LearningOutcomeItem (lookup): Competencies map to learning objectives\n- PersonTrait -> Account (child): Traits belong to a student\n- PersonAffinity -> Account (child): Affinities belong to a student\n- CareCoordinator -> Account (child): Care coordinators are assigned to students\n- CareRole -> CareCoordinator (parent): Roles are assigned to care coordinator records\n- Case -> Account (child): Cases are filed for a student\n- Product2 -> PricebookEntry (parent): Products have price book entries for term-specific pricing\n- Pricebook2 -> PricebookEntry (parent): Price books contain price book entries\n- PricebookEntry -> Product2 (child): Entries belong to a product\n- PricebookEntry -> Pricebook2 (child): Entries belong to a price book\n- Order -> Account (child): Orders belong to a student\n- Order -> OrderProduct (parent): Orders contain line items\n- Order -> AcademicTerm (lookup): Orders reference the academic term\n- OrderProduct -> Order (child): Line items belong to an order\n- OrderProduct -> Product2 (lookup): Line items reference a fee product\n- Program -> MentoringProfile (parent): Programs have mentor and mentee profiles\n- Program -> Benefit (parent): Programs define available benefits\n- MentoringProfile -> Program (child): Profiles belong to a mentoring program\n- MentoringProfile -> Account (lookup): Profiles reference a person account\n- Benefit -> Program (child): Benefits belong to a mentoring program\n- Benefit -> BenefitAssignment (parent): Benefits are assigned through assignment records\n- BenefitAssignment -> Benefit (child): Assignments reference a benefit type\n- BenefitAssignment -> MentoringProfile (lookup): Assignments link mentor and mentee profiles\n- GiftTransaction -> Account (child): Gift transactions belong to a donor\n- GiftTransaction -> GiftCommitment (lookup): Transactions may fulfill a pledge commitment\n- GiftCommitment -> Account (child): Commitments belong to a donor\n- GiftCommitment -> GiftTransaction (parent): Commitments are fulfilled by gift transactions\n- VolunteerActivity -> Account (child): Volunteer activities belong to an alumni\n- ServiceAppointment -> Account (child): Appointments are booked for students\n- ServiceAppointment -> ServiceTerritory (lookup): Appointments are assigned to a service territory\n- ServiceAppointment -> ServiceResource (lookup): Appointments are assigned to a staff member\n- ServiceResource -> ServiceTerritory (lookup): Resources are assigned to service territories\n- ServiceResource -> ServiceAppointment (parent): Resources receive appointment assignments\n- ServiceTerritory -> ServiceResource (parent): Territories have assigned service resources\n- ServiceTerritory -> ServiceAppointment (parent): Appointments are booked within territories\n- DataStream -> IdentityResolutionRuleset (lookup): Data streams feed into identity resolution rulesets\n- IdentityResolutionRuleset -> MatchRule (parent): Rulesets contain matching rules\n- MatchRule -> IdentityResolutionRuleset (child): Match rules belong to a ruleset\n- UnifiedIndividual -> Account (lookup): Unified individuals map to person accounts\n\n## Cross-Domain Connections\n- Recruitment <-> Admissions: Qualified prospects move to applications\n- Recruitment <-> Student Records: Prospect data creates person records\n- Recruitment <-> Agentforce: AI recruitment agent automates outreach\n- Recruitment <-> Experience Cloud: Prospect portal for inquiry and events\n- Recruitment <-> Education Analytics: Recruitment funnel dashboards\n- Admissions <-> Student Records: Applicant data stored on person records\n- Admissions <-> Enrollment: Admitted students enroll in programs\n- Admissions <-> Student Financials: Application fees and financial aid packaging\n- Admissions <-> Agentforce: AI agent answers applicant questions\n- Admissions <-> Experience Cloud: Applicant portal for status tracking\n- Academic Operations <-> Enrollment: Programs and courses define what students enroll in\n- Academic Operations <-> Student Records: Transfer credits link to student education history\n- Academic Operations <-> Student Financials: Course structure drives tuition calculation\n- Academic Operations <-> Student Success: Learning objectives tie to competency tracking\n- Enrollment <-> Student Records: Enrollment records link to person accounts\n- Enrollment <-> Student Success: Enrollment progress feeds student success tracking\n- Enrollment <-> Student Financials: Course registration triggers fee calculation\n- Enrollment <-> Appointments: Registration may require advising appointments\n- Student Records <-> Student Success: Competencies and traits linked to person\n- Student Records <-> Student Financials: Financial accounts linked to person records\n- Student Records <-> Mentoring: Mentor/mentee profiles reference person records\n- Student Records <-> Alumni & Advancement: Alumni status tracked on person records\n- Student Records <-> Appointments: Appointments reference student records\n- Student Records <-> Data 360: Identity resolution unifies duplicate person records\n- Student Success <-> Mentoring: Mentoring outcomes feed into success tracking\n- Student Success <-> Appointments: Advising appointments support interventions\n- Student Success <-> Agentforce: AI advising agent surfaces at-risk students\n- Student Financials <-> Alumni & Advancement: Scholarship funds connect to advancement\n- Student Financials <-> Agentforce: Financial aid agent answers billing questions\n- Student Financials <-> Experience Cloud: Student financial self-service portal\n- Student Financials <-> Education Analytics: Financial health dashboards\n- Mentoring <-> Appointments: Mentoring sessions scheduled as appointments\n- Alumni & Advancement <-> Agentforce: Alumni engagement agent manages outreach\n- Alumni & Advancement <-> Experience Cloud: Alumni portal for giving and volunteering\n- Alumni & Advancement <-> Education Analytics: Advancement dashboards and giving reports\n- Education Analytics <-> Agentforce: Agent performance monitoring\n\n## Data Flows\n### Recruitment\n1. Import unengaged suspects as Lead records and capture their source and status.\n2. Capture requests for information through the Education Portal, and use the Student Recruitment Agent for automated questions, recruiter-contact requests, and campus tour registration.\n3. Convert interested suspects into Person Accounts and retain related ContactProfile data as part of the person record.\n4. Organize prospects with campaigns and Actionable Lists, then assign outreach work to recruitment staff.\n5. Review recruitment funnel dashboards and prospect engagement predictions to prioritize follow-up.\n6. Advance qualified prospects into admissions applications.\n### Admissions\n1. Start applications for recruited prospects already represented by Person Accounts.\n2. Let applicants use the Education Portal to complete tasks, track status, and ask the AI agent admissions questions.\n3. Create Preliminary Application Reference and Individual Application records, collect documents, and process applicable application fees.\n4. Verify application requirements, assign reviews, score applications, and submit recommendations.\n5. Create and release the admissions decision, and provide financial aid packaging information.\n6. Enroll admitted students in the selected learning program, learning program plan, and academic term.\n### Academic Operations\n1. Define Academic Years, Academic Terms, and Academic Sessions.\n2. Create Learning Courses, Learning Programs, requirements, and learning outcomes.\n3. Build and publish Learning Program Plans, Course Offerings, and schedules.\n4. Use Learning Programs and Learning Courses for enrollment and tuition calculation.\n5. Evaluate Course Credit Transfer Applications and link approved credit to education history.\n6. Feed learning outcomes and program plans into competency and learner-progress tracking.\n### Enrollment\n1. Record the admission decision and link the learner’s records to a person account.\n2. Enroll the admitted learner in a program, program plan, and academic term.\n3. Open course registration and evaluate eligibility, holds, schedules, and any required advising.\n4. Register or waitlist the learner and trigger applicable fee calculation.\n5. Record course results and learning achievements, then calculate and publish academic scores.\n6. Update Learner Pathways and Learner Progress for degree planning and student success tracking.\n### Student Records\n1. Create Person Accounts from prospect data and enrich them with applicant information during recruitment and admissions.\n2. Add contact points, household relationships, demographics, languages, education history, examinations, and employment to the person record.\n3. Use education history for transfer credit evaluation and reference the same student identity from enrollment and academic records.\n4. Link student success competencies and traits, financial accounts, mentoring profiles, and appointment records to the person.\n5. Track alumni status and engagement on the continuing person record after the learner completes their studies.\n6. Resolve duplicate identities with Data 360 and harmonize institutional data into a unified learner profile.\n### Student Success\n1. Unify learner competencies, traits, affinities, goals, and success metrics on person records.\n2. Bring course performance, learning outcomes, and mentoring outcomes into success tracking.\n3. Evaluate grade and GPA conditions, and use the Course Operations Agent to identify at-risk learners.\n4. Create Record Alerts and advising Cases, then assign success teams, care plans, tasks, and benefits.\n5. Schedule advising appointments and use Student Advising Topics to summarize cases and suggest next-best actions.\n6. Review analytics and student success KPIs, then record intervention progress and outcomes.\n### Student Financials\n1. Define academic programs and courses, and model tuition, application fees, and other charges as Product2 records.\n2. Group fee products in Pricebook2 records, set PricebookEntry prices, and associate price books with academic intervals.\n3. Trigger charge creation from admissions or registration activity, and create an Order with its OrderProduct records.\n4. Create a separate Academic Order to link the Order to the applicable academic session, term, or year.\n5. Activate the Order to generate billing schedules and invoices, then collect payments or apply credit memos.\n6. Link financial accounts and aid data to person records, and expose balances, aid packages, agents, self-service, and financial health dashboards.\n### Mentoring\n1. Define the Program and configure its mentoring dates, status, participation details, and related support structure.\n2. Create the Benefit for the Program and configure its mentoring type, description, frequency, and availability.\n3. Enroll mentors and mentees, collect assessment responses, and create MentoringProfile records linked to their person records.\n4. Compare eligible mentors with the mentee and set the Provider on the Benefit Assignment to the selected mentor's contact value.\n5. Schedule mentoring sessions as appointments and track interactions, alerts, tasks, assessments, and related activity.\n6. Evaluate mentoring outcomes with Outcome Management and track the results as student success metrics.\n### Alumni & Advancement\n1. Represent alumni with Person Accounts and track alumni status with Constituent Role records.\n2. Engage alumni through Experience Cloud profiles, directories, volunteer opportunities, and donor self-service.\n3. Launch fundraising campaigns, capture gifts, and process Gift Commitments and their schedules.\n4. Prepare contributed funds for institutional accounting through Accounting Subledger.\n5. Unify engagement and giving data in Data 360, then review Alumni Engagement Metrics, dashboards, giving reports, and Donor Engagement Agent outputs.\n### Appointments\n1. Configure appointment topics, engagement channels, service territories, success teams, service resources, and shifts.\n2. Reference the learner's person record and related case, including advising required before registration when applicable.\n3. Present eligible staff, channels, locations, and time slots through the Education Cloud scheduling experience.\n4. Book the appointment for student success, advising, or mentoring, or check the learner into a separate drop-in waitlist.\n5. Create the Service Appointment after scheduling or after a service resource accepts a drop-in check-in.\n6. Track the appointment and let eligible attendees join the configured virtual call when applicable.\n### Education Analytics\n1. Enable Education Analytics and assign the required CRM Analytics permission sets.\n2. Refresh Education Analytics data from Education Cloud and its connected recruitment funnel records.\n3. Analyze recruitment funnel dashboards and student success KPIs in CRM Analytics.\n4. Review connected revenue and financial aid analytics and advancement and giving dashboards.\n5. Monitor Agentforce performance alongside institutional analytics.\n6. Embed the appropriate dashboards in Education Cloud app consoles for each role.\n### Agentforce\n1. Enable Education Cloud, Einstein Generative AI, Agentforce, and the required domain features.\n2. Assign agent, user, object, field, flow, and data-access permissions.\n3. Configure knowledge, data streams, search retrievers, prompt templates, subagents, actions, and escalation paths.\n4. Deploy agents through Experience Cloud messaging, employee channels, or Slack as appropriate.\n5. Handle recruitment outreach, application-status questions, at-risk learner support, financial aid guidance, and alumni engagement follow-up.\n6. Monitor agent consumption, audit information, usage metrics, and analytics dashboards.\n### Experience Cloud\n1. Configure the Education Portal template and add the required Experience Cloud pages and components.\n2. Assign licenses, permission sets, audiences, and sharing access for each constituent role.\n3. Expose recruitment inquiry and event experiences and route prospects into admissions application tracking.\n4. Enable student course-registration and financial self-service features when the required add-ons and permissions are configured.\n5. Provide alumni profile and directory access through the alumni community portal.\n6. Preview and publish the portal for authorized desktop and mobile users.\n### Data 360\n1. Connect institutional source systems to Data 360.\n2. Deploy and activate data streams in the appropriate data space.\n3. Map source objects and fields to the required data model objects.\n4. Configure and run identity resolution rules to unify student person records.\n5. Use unified learner profiles for insights, analytics, automation, and trusted AI grounding.\n\n## Links\nUse these URLs when mentioning domains or components:\n- [Recruitment](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/recruitment)\n- [Admissions](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/admissions)\n- [Academic Operations](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/academics)\n- [Enrollment](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/enrollment)\n- [Student Records](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/records)\n- [Student Success](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/success)\n- [Student Financials](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/financials)\n- [Mentoring](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/mentoring)\n- [Alumni & Advancement](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/alumni)\n- [Appointments](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/appointments)\n- [Education Analytics](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/analytics)\n- [Agentforce](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/agentforce)\n- [Experience Cloud](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/experience)\n- [Data 360](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/educationcloud/#/data360)\n";
